solverMLE -- Maximum likelihood estimate of a loopless mixed graph
******************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        solverMLE(G,U)
  * Inputs:
      * G, a *note graph: (Graphs)Graph,, or a *note digraph: (Graphs)Digraph,,
        or an instance of the type *note Bigraph: (StatGraphs)Bigraph,, or an
        instance of the type *note MixedGraph: (StatGraphs)MixedGraph,
      * U, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, or a *note list:
        (Macaulay2Doc)List, of sample data. Alternatively, the sample
        covariance matrix can be given as input by setting *note SampleData:
        SampleData, to false
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note ConcentrationMatrix:
        solverMLE_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmConcentrationMatrix=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value false, output MLE for concentration matrix instead of MLE
        for covariance matrix
      * *note OptionsEigenSolver:
        solverMLE_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmOptionsEigenSolver=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value new OptionTable from {Strategy => Stickelberger,
        Tolerance => .000001, Basis => null, Multiplier => 0}, use options of
        "zeroDimSolve" in "EigenSolver"
      * *note OptionsNAG4M2: solverMLE_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmOptionsNAG4M2=>_pd_pd_pd_
        rp, => ..., default value new OptionTable from {EndZoneFactor => null,
        maxCorrSteps => null, Predictor => null, stepIncreaseFactor => null,
        Precision => null, tStep => null, tDegree => null, CorrectorTolerance
        => null, InfinityThreshold => null, gamma => null, Software => null,
        ResidualTolerance => null, numberSuccessesBeforeIncrease => null,
        Normalize => null, SingularConditionNumber => null, Projectivize =>
        null, PostProcess => true, tStepMin => null}, use options of
        "solveSystem" in "NumericalAlgebraicGeometry"
      * *note RealPrecision: solverMLE_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmRealPrecision=>_pd_pd_pd_
        rp, => ..., default value 53, the number of bits of precision to use in
        the computation
      * *note SampleData: solverMLE_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmSampleData=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, =>
        ..., default value true, input sample covariance matrix instead of
        sample data
      * *note Saturate: solverMLE_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmSaturate=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value true, whether to saturate
      * *note SaturateOptions:
        solverMLE_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmSaturateOptions=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ..., default
        value new OptionTable from {Strategy => null, BasisElementLimit =>
        infinity, DegreeLimit => {}, MinimalGenerators => true, PairLimit =>
        infinity}, use options from "saturate"
      * *note Solver: solverMLE_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmSolver=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value "EigenSolver", choose numerical solver
      * *note ZeroTolerance: solverMLE_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmZeroTolerance=>_pd_pd_pd_
        rp, => ..., default value 1e-10, optional input to set the largest
        absolute value that should be treated as zero
  * Outputs:
      * a *note sequence: (Macaulay2Doc)Sequence,, of length 3, whose first
        element is the maximum value attained in the log-likelihood function
        (of type *note RR: (Macaulay2Doc)RR,), its second element is the MLE
        (or MLEs) of the covariance matrix (of types *note Matrix:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix, or *note List: (Macaulay2Doc)List,), and its
        third element is the ML-degree of the model (of type *note ZZ:
        (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,).  By providing true as the value for the option
        *note ConcentrationMatrix: ConcentrationMatrix,, the MLE for the
        concentration matrix can be given as output.

Description
===========

This function takes as input a *note graph: (Graphs)Graph,, or a *note digraph:
(Graphs)Digraph,, or a *note bigraph: (StatGraphs)Bigraph, or a *note mixed
graph: (StatGraphs)MixedGraph, and a list or matrix that encodes, by default,
the sample data. It computes the critical points of the score equations and
selects the maximum value achieved among those that lie in the cone of
positive-definite matrices. The default output is the maximum value in the
log-likelihood function, maximum likelihood estimators (MLE) for the covariance
matrix and the ML-degree of the model. MLE for the concentration matrix can be
obtained by setting the optional input *note ConcentrationMatrix:
ConcentrationMatrix, to false.

The same optional inputs as in *note scoreEquations: scoreEquations, can be
used, plus extra optional inputs related to the numerical solver (EigenSolver
by default, NAG4M2 alternatively) and its functionalities.

Below we reproduce Example 2.1.13 for the 4-cycle in the book: Mathias Drton,
Bernd Sturmfels and Seth Sullivant, Lectures on Algebraic Statistics,
Oberwolfach Seminars, Vol 40, Birkhauser, Basel, 2009.



+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : G=graph{{1,2},{2,3},{3,4},{1,4}};                            |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : U =matrix{{1,2,1,-1},{2,1,3,0},{-1, 0, 1, 1},{-5, 3, 4, -6}};|
|                                                                  |
|              4       4                                           |
|o2 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ                                            |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : solverMLE(G,U)                                               |
|                                                                  |
|o3 = (-6.2615, | 7.1875   -1.625   -2.22576 5.875    |, 5)        |
|               | -1.625   1.25     .875     -1.62833 |            |
|               | -2.22576 .875     1.6875   -2.625   |            |
|               | 5.875    -1.62833 -2.625   7.25     |            |
|                                                                  |
|o3 : Sequence                                                     |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+

The data sample can also be given as a list:



+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : G=graph{{1,2},{2,3},{3,4},{1,4}};                       |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : U = {{1,2,1,-1},{2,1,3,0},{-1, 0, 1, 1},{-5, 3, 4, -6}};|
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : solverMLE(G,U)                                          |
|                                                             |
|o6 = (-6.2615, | 7.1875   -1.625   -2.22576 5.875    |, 5)   |
|               | -1.625   1.25     .875     -1.62833 |       |
|               | -2.22576 .875     1.6875   -2.625   |       |
|               | 5.875    -1.62833 -2.625   7.25     |       |
|                                                             |
|o6 : Sequence                                                |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+

In the following example we compute the MLE for the covariance matrix of the
graphical model associated to the graph $1\rightarrow 2,1\rightarrow
3,2\rightarrow 3,3\rightarrow 4,3<-> 4$ In this case we give as input the
sample covariance matrix:



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : G = mixedGraph(digraph {{1,3},{2,4}},bigraph {{3,4}});                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : S =  matrix {{7/20, 13/50, -3/50, -19/100}, {13/50, 73/100, -7/100,     |
|                                                                             |
|              4       4                                                      |
|o8 : Matrix QQ  <-- QQ                                                       |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|-9/100},{-3/50, -7/100, 2/5, 3/50}, {-19/100, -9/100, 3/50, 59/100}};        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : solverMLE(G,S,SampleData=>false)                                        |
|                                                                             |
|o9 = (-1.14351, | .35          0            -.0523685   0         |, 5)      |
|                | 7.50541e-20  .73          -1.2436e-18 -.0877801 |          |
|                | -.0523685    -6.87648e-19 .397716     .027832   |          |
|                | -1.02256e-18 -.0877801    .027832     .589466   |          |
|                                                                             |
|o9 : Sequence                                                                |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Next we provide the MLE for the concentration matrix of the graphical model
associated to the graph $1\rightarrow 3,2\rightarrow 4,3<->4,1 - 2$. Again the
sample covariance matrix is given as input.



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : G = mixedGraph(digraph {{1,3},{2,4}},bigraph {{3,4}},graph {{1,2}});   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : S =  matrix {{7/20, 13/50, -3/50, -19/100}, {13/50, 73/100, -7/100,    |
|                                                                             |
|               4       4                                                     |
|o11 : Matrix QQ  <-- QQ                                                      |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|-9/100},{-3/50, -7/100, 2/5, 3/50}, {-19/100, -9/100, 3/50, 59/100}};        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i12 : solverMLE(G,S,SampleData=>false,ConcentrationMatrix=>true)             |
|                                                                             |
|o12 = (-.836195, | 3.94266   -1.38594  .385091   -.0185138 |, 5)             |
|                 | -1.38594  1.88776   -.0148788 .208427   |                 |
|                 | .385091   -.0148788 2.57372   -.123735  |                 |
|                 | -.0185138 .208427   -.123735  1.73333   |                 |
|                                                                             |
|o12 : Sequence                                                               |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Application to positive definite matrix completion problems

Consider the following symmetric matrix with some unknown entries:

+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i13 : R=QQ[x,y];                                                     |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i14 : M=matrix{{115,-13,x,47},{-13,5,7,y},{x,7,27,-21},{47,y,-21,29}}|
|                                                                     |
|o14 = | 115 -13 x   47  |                                            |
|      | -13 5   7   y   |                                            |
|      | x   7   27  -21 |                                            |
|      | 47  y   -21 29  |                                            |
|                                                                     |
|              4      4                                               |
|o14 : Matrix R  <-- R                                                |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+

Unknown entries correspond to non-edges of the 4-cycle. A positive definite
completion of this matrix is obtained by giving values to x and y and computing
the MLE for the covariance matrix in the Gaussian graphical model given by the
4-cycle. To understand which values of x and y will result in a maximum
likelihood estimate, see Example 12.16 in the book: Mateusz Michalek and Bernd
Sturmfels, Invitation to Nonlinear Algebra, Graduate Studies in Mathematics,
Vol ???, American Mathematical Society, 2021.



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i15 : G=graph{{1,2},{2,3},{3,4},{1,4}};                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i16 : V=matrix{{115,-13,-29,47},{-13,5,7,-11},{-29,7,27,-21},{47,-11,-21,29}}|
|                                                                             |
|o16 = | 115 -13 -29 47  |                                                    |
|      | -13 5   7   -11 |                                                    |
|      | -29 7   27  -21 |                                                    |
|      | 47  -11 -21 29  |                                                    |
|                                                                             |
|               4       4                                                     |
|o16 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ                                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i17 : (mx,MLE,ML)=solverMLE(G,V,SampleData=>false)                           |
|                                                                             |
|o17 = (-14.5793, | 115      -13      -35.6122 47       |, 5)                 |
|                 | -13      5        7        -6.51331 |                     |
|                 | -35.6122 7        27       -21      |                     |
|                 | 47       -6.51331 -21      29       |                     |
|                                                                             |
|o17 : Sequence                                                               |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

The MLE of the covariance matrix is the unique positive definite completion of
the matrix M such that its inverse, namely the concentration matrix, has zero's
in the entries corresponding to non-edges of the graph. Observe that all
entries of V remain the same in the MLE except for those that correspond to
non-edges of the graph.



